course: dictionary
lesson: 2847
title: Documentary: real life on film
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A documentary is a film or TV programme about real people, places and events. It gives facts and information. «Documentaries are films about real life, like nature, history or sport.»
explain: Documentary can also be an adjective, meaning made of documents. «Documentary evidence» is proof written down, such as letters or records. The context tells you which meaning is being used.
explain: Real does not always mean the whole story. «Even true documentaries are made, not just recorded.» Makers choose what to show. «Documentary shows about real hospitals film real patients who agreed; they show the exciting moments chosen from hundreds of hours.» So ask: what was chosen, and what was left out?
explain: Use the context to check. Your meaning should fit the sentence. «Even a documentary can be staged, so it is worth asking how a scene was filmed.» Staged means arranged on purpose, so a film can look real even when a scene was set up.
fact: A documentary is a film or TV programme about real people, places and events.
fact: Documentaries give facts and information about real life, such as nature, history or sport.
fact: As an adjective, «documentary» means made of documents, as in «documentary evidence».
fact: Documentaries are made, not just recorded, so makers can choose what to show.
fact: «Staged» means arranged on purpose, so a documentary scene may not have happened exactly as it looks.
exercise: «Documentaries are films about real life, like nature, history or sport.» What is a documentary? | A book of poems | *A film or TV programme about real people, places and events | A cartoon for very young children | A made-up story with a made-up hero
exercise: «Documentary evidence» means proof that is | Filmed on a phone only | *Written down, such as letters or records | Painted on a wall | A spoken story passed on from grandparents
exercise: «Even true documentaries are made, not just recorded.» What does this tell you? | Documentaries are never filmed on real days | Documentaries have no facts in them | Every documentary is a lie | *Makers choose what to show, so you should think about what you see
test: What tells you a documentary is about real life? | It is always filmed in a studio | *Its people and events are real | The actors are famous | It always has a happy ending
test: «Even a documentary can be staged, so it is worth asking how a scene was filmed.» What is the best question to ask? | Is the sun shining today? | How many cameras did they own? | *How was this scene set up and filmed? | Who wrote the music?
exam: Which is the best way to think about a documentary? | Believe every single thing you see | *Ask what was chosen to show, and what was left out | Stop watching when it gets exciting | Trust the title and skip the rest
